"""
Machu Picchu (Q676203) — the Inca citadel on its ridge, with its terraces, temples and houses, built by code.

Author: Álvaro C. Chulvi (with AI assistance). Licence of the resulting model: CC BY 4.0.
The monument: built for the Inca Pachacútec around 1450 (Wikidata P571 ~1450), abandoned in the 16th century;
no known architect. Public domain.

What is modelled:
- The terraces (andenes): the 161 retaining walls drawn by OpenStreetMap mappers inside the archaeological site —
  the agricultural terraces of the south-east slope below the Guardhouse, the terraces of the east slope, around
  the Intihuatana hill, below the Sacred Plaza, at the north end towards Huayna Picchu and inside the Main
  Plaza — each as a granite wall going down into the slope with its flat grass terrace behind it (a grey coping
  on the edge, the grass up to the foot of the next wall up).
- The urban sector: the 98 buildings OpenStreetMap draws inside the site (houses, kanchas, temples, storehouses),
  as roofless granite walls ~0.8 m thick with their gable ends (hastiales), trapezoidal doors (one in a long side)
  and trapezoidal windows; the large enclosures as plain walls. Every building stands on its own floor, its
  walls going down 2 m below the lowest ground at its corners.
- The Sacred Plaza: the Main Temple and the Temple of the Three Windows (three-walled, open to the plaza; the
  three big trapezoidal windows look east over the Main Plaza), the Priest's House and the House of the
  Ornaments; the Temple of the Sun (Torreón) on its granite boulder with the Royal Tomb (a dark cave) beneath
  and its two trapezoidal windows; the Intihuatana stone on the top of its terraced hill (raised 5 m above the
  smoothed SRTM ground, so the hill reads), the Sacred Rock at the north end of the Main Plaza.
- The Guardhouse (at the top of the agricultural terraces) and the five storehouses (qolqas) by the
  agricultural sector, with their reconstructed thatched roofs; the free-standing walls (city wall, enclosure
  walls) and the stone staircases (as OpenStreetMap draws them, except the long trails to Huayna Picchu,
  Intipunku and the Inca bridge).
- The ground of the plazas, yards and slopes among the ruins, laid 0.25 m over the ground (since 2026-11-28 all of
  it, not only what OSM tags as grass: before, the game saw through the rest and drew its own ground there): grass
  where OSM has landuse=grass, the trails and footpaths of OSM as earth or stone (model_sources/Q676203_ground_osm.json),
  darker green on its scrub and wood, and worn lawn (grass and earth) on the untagged ground; the quarry (the
  «Caos Granítico», OSM node 12940704693) as bare granite with a field of loose boulders (sizes estimated).
- Not modelled: Huayna Picchu and Machu Picchu mountains, the bare terrain, the modern buildings (the control
  huts by Huayna Picchu, the Sanctuary Lodge, the ticket and bus station), the tourist paths, the Inca trail
  and the Inca bridge, the Funerary Rock.

Measurements (see meta.json for the sources):
- Plan: OpenStreetMap — the archaeological site (way 157376572, cut at 262 m south of the origin, before the
  hotel), its buildings (building=ruins/yes), retaining walls (barrier=retaining_wall), walls (barrier=wall),
  steps (highway=steps) and grass (landuse=grass); named nodes for the temples (Templo del Sol 874540057, Tumba
  Real 874540031, Intihuatana 874540036, Templo Principal 4621573692, Templo de las Tres Ventanas 4621576989,
  Casa del sacerdote 13198843222, Casa de los Ornamentos 13198843226, Roca Sagrada 9726879350). The Guardhouse is
  building way 480263540, the storehouses the five buildings around 165 m south-east of the City Gate.
- Ground: SRTM 30 m (NASA, via OpenTopoData) on a 15 m grid, embedded below; y = 0 is the ground at the origin in
  the middle of the Main Plaza (≈ 2440 m in SRTM; the usual figure for the site is 2430 m). SRTM smooths the
  ridge, so the terraces get their levels from it: each terrace is level with the ground at the foot of the
  next wall up (+0.35 m), each wall goes down 2.5 m below the ground at its foot. The agricultural terraces go
  down to y ≈ −30 on the south-east, the east terraces to y ≈ −45, the Guardhouse stands ≈ 20 m above the plaza.
- Heights (estimates, ±25 %, from photographs and the usual descriptions: Inca houses with walls ~2.5–3 m to the
  eaves and steep gables, windows and doors trapezoidal): houses 2.4–3.1 m to the eaves, gables to 0.7 × the
  width (≤ 3.4 m); Main Temple and Three Windows walls 3.8 m (Three Windows: windows 1.5 m high, 1.2 m wide at the
  sill, 0.9 m at the lintel); Torreón 4.2 m on its boulder; terrace walls as the ground gives them (1–4 m).
Grey-white granite, green grass, straw thatch (ichu).

Axes (docs/MODEL_SPEC.md): +Y up, +Z points to heading 157.2° — from the middle of the Main Plaza towards the
Guardhouse — so the default view (az 0) is the classic one, from the Guardhouse looking north-north-west across
the city to Huayna Picchu. +X points to 67.2° (east-north-east, the east slope). Origin on the ground in the middle
of the Main Plaza.

    python model_sources/Q676203_machu_picchu.py   -> models/Q676203/model.glb (+ triangles, footprint in meta.json)
"""
